Stop paying AVIF's codec load inside the first encode

A first AVIF appeared to take ~9s when the encode itself was under 2s. The
difference was libavif's ~3.5MB binary being downloaded and compiled inside
the encode, and being counted in the reported duration.

- Instantiate the selected codec when it is selected, not on first use.
- Once images are queued, pull the other codecs into cache in the background
  with compileStreaming, which warms the engine's wasm code cache as well as
  the HTTP cache. Needed because the format bar only exists once there is
  something to compress, so warming on selection alone always loses the race
  against the re-encode that selection triggers. A plain fetch was not enough:
  a response whose body is never read can be cancelled before it completes.
- Load libavif's glue from /codecs at runtime instead of bundling it, dropping
  ~1MB of emscripten glue from the client bundle.

Threading was attempted and does not work. jSquash selects the multi-threaded
codecs whenever SharedArrayBuffer exists, so adding COOP/COEP is enough to
enable them -- and AVIF then hangs forever. The MT module pre-allocates one
pthread worker per core at init and blocks readiness until all report back;
from inside a nested worker they never do, silently, without even requesting
its wasm. Verified with the glue served as a real URL and with
mainScriptUrlOrBlob passed explicitly; the same factory resolves in ~60ms on
the main thread. loadAvif now pins the single-threaded build explicitly rather
than feature-detecting, so enabling isolation later cannot silently break it.

Also:
- Pin OxiPNG to its single-threaded build for the same reason, driving the
  wasm-bindgen module directly rather than through a wrapper that picks a
  build at runtime but takes one wasm URL for both.
- Show "collapsing..." while re-encoding instead of the previous result, which
  reported a stale size as though it were the new one.
- Stop linting the generated codec glue staged into public/codecs.

